WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.967 --> 00:00:02.135
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:03.503
We're here with Bob Odenkirk.

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:04.704
I have spent -- because my
wife's family is from there,

00:00:04.704 --> 00:00:06.573
I've spent a lot of time
in Albuquerque.

00:00:06.573 --> 00:00:07.906
It's a very beautiful place.

00:00:07.906 --> 00:00:12.011
I haven't spent a lot of time
in the summer months.

00:00:12.011 --> 00:00:14.714
And I feel like you had
some real desert work

00:00:14.714 --> 00:00:16.915
over the last couple of seasons.
-Yeah, season five,

00:00:16.915 --> 00:00:18.585
there's a huge episode

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:20.520
that takes place entirely
in the desert.

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.121
Do you know it, anybody?
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:22.121 --> 00:00:25.925
Me and Jonathan Banks, Mike,
we're out in the desert.

00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:28.661
We're trying to -- We're getting
chased by some people.

00:00:28.661 --> 00:00:31.831
And we actually shot for 14 days

00:00:31.831 --> 00:00:35.000
in 110-degree heat
in the desert.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.470
And I'll tell you what.
Here's how hot it was.

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:40.139
When we shoot outside
in Albuquerque

00:00:40.139 --> 00:00:43.242
and the surrounding areas,
we have a snake wrangler

00:00:43.242 --> 00:00:47.079
who comes out and checks around
and finds the little fellas.

00:00:47.079 --> 00:00:50.016
They're everywhere.
And puts them in a bag for you.

00:00:50.016 --> 00:00:51.617
And then holds onto them
while you act

00:00:51.617 --> 00:00:53.253
and then let's them go back
to their business.

00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:54.553
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.553 --> 00:00:57.823
'Cause snakes don't want you
acting on their land.

00:00:57.823 --> 00:00:59.358
-They have no appreciation
for the art form.

00:00:59.358 --> 00:01:01.928
-They don't care about
thespianism.

00:01:01.928 --> 00:01:03.897
[ Laughter ]

00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.399
And so
the snake wrangler's working,

00:01:06.399 --> 00:01:09.402
and he's not finding any snakes,

00:01:09.402 --> 00:01:10.903
which is kind of concerning,
right?

00:01:10.903 --> 00:01:13.273
Like, we don't have
a good snake wrangler, maybe.

00:01:13.273 --> 00:01:17.009
And we go ask him, "Why are
there no snakes in your bag?"

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:19.746
And he goes,
"It's too hot for snakes."

00:01:19.746 --> 00:01:22.047
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.047 --> 00:01:23.716
That's pretty hot.
-Yeah.

00:01:23.716 --> 00:01:26.953
-When the snakes are like,
"Yeah, no, no, not --"

00:01:26.953 --> 00:01:28.054
[ Laughter ]

00:01:28.054 --> 00:01:30.456
-You worked at "SNL"
as a writer.

00:01:30.456 --> 00:01:32.825
-Yeah.
-And I think a lot of people --

00:01:32.825 --> 00:01:35.695
I certainly remember the first
time I met Lorne Michaels.

00:01:35.695 --> 00:01:38.364
And you, in your head,
have this burden of,

00:01:38.364 --> 00:01:40.766
"I hope I don't say anything
that might blow it,

00:01:40.766 --> 00:01:42.268
'cause I've gotten this close."

00:01:42.268 --> 00:01:43.670
You played it
slightly differently.

00:01:43.670 --> 00:01:45.437
-Well, yeah.

00:01:45.437 --> 00:01:48.474
Well, Lorne is a busy man.

00:01:48.474 --> 00:01:51.544
And oftentimes, when it's your
first time to be interviewed,

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:53.012
you do have a waiting period.

00:01:53.012 --> 00:01:54.647
-There's a bit of waiting
on the couch outside.

00:01:54.647 --> 00:01:56.181
-A couple hours.
-Yeah.

00:01:56.181 --> 00:01:58.851
-And for me, it was about
an hour and 45 minutes.

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:02.956
And that time was not good
to me, because I was, like,

00:02:02.956 --> 00:02:05.925
thinking, "How do I work this?
I want this job.

00:02:05.925 --> 00:02:08.194
How do I get this?
You know what?

00:02:08.194 --> 00:02:10.529
This guy doesn't
want his ass kissed."

00:02:10.529 --> 00:02:13.132
[ Laughter ]
"He's had that.

00:02:13.132 --> 00:02:15.101
He doesn't need to hear that.

00:02:15.101 --> 00:02:17.136
He wants to hear there's
a critical mind at work."

00:02:17.136 --> 00:02:18.571
[ Laughter ]

00:02:18.571 --> 00:02:20.807
So, "It's your turn,
Mr. Odenkirk,

00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:23.943
you little nothing guy
from Chicago,

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:27.180
who's not even a waiter yet."

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:30.549
They wouldn't even let me wait
on tables at the restaurant.

00:02:30.549 --> 00:02:33.686
And I come in, and I'm like --
Lorne's like,

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:37.589
"Well, now, uh, what do you
think of the show?"

00:02:37.589 --> 00:02:39.192
And I'm like, "It's not great."

00:02:39.192 --> 00:02:41.861
[ Laughter ]
"It's not -- It's not great."

00:02:41.861 --> 00:02:45.397
But without the smile.
I'm totally serious.

00:02:45.397 --> 00:02:47.800
"Yeah, I don't like it.
I like 'Monty Python.'

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:49.802
Yeah, that's better.
Better."

00:02:49.802 --> 00:02:52.004
"Well, you know, we're thinking
about having writers here."

00:02:52.004 --> 00:02:55.808
"Okay. Well, sure, the show
could be a lot better."

00:02:55.808 --> 00:02:58.911
You know, I'm critical,
very harsh,

00:02:58.911 --> 00:03:00.613
and for some reason,
he still hired me."

00:03:00.613 --> 00:03:01.880
-It's crazy.
It worked.

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:04.217
-I think he meets a lot of
crazy young people.

00:03:04.217 --> 00:03:07.152
-Yeah, I think that's true,
and I do think --

00:03:07.152 --> 00:03:09.454
I mean, I think youth
and a little bit of craziness

00:03:09.454 --> 00:03:10.756
are actually very key.

00:03:10.756 --> 00:03:12.659
-Yeah, especially
in a comedy writer.

00:03:12.659 --> 00:03:13.692
-Yes.

00:03:13.692 --> 00:03:14.960
-They should be
a little unhinged.

00:03:14.960 --> 00:03:17.129
-Well, because this is
really interesting to me.

00:03:17.129 --> 00:03:19.064
So one of the --
I love "Mr. Show,"

00:03:19.064 --> 00:03:20.933
which is your fantastic,
you and David Cross,

00:03:20.933 --> 00:03:22.235
your fantastic sketch show.
-Yeah.

00:03:22.235 --> 00:03:24.803
Everything I learned
at "Saturday Night Live,"

00:03:24.803 --> 00:03:27.606
I got to use making "Mr. Show."

00:03:27.606 --> 00:03:30.409
-There was, I will say,
a fascinating thing you said

00:03:30.409 --> 00:03:33.446
about writers bringing in ideas
and that you would not

00:03:33.446 --> 00:03:36.015
hear an idea and dismiss it
as being a bad idea.

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:37.416
-No.
-You as a group of very talented

00:03:37.416 --> 00:03:40.219
writers would just drill down
and find the comedy in it.

00:03:40.219 --> 00:03:44.790
-To a torturous extent,
if somebody pitched an idea,

00:03:44.790 --> 00:03:49.228
any idea, we would work it over
until we found something.

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:54.567
And I was tenacious and
unpleasant in pushing it.

00:03:54.567 --> 00:03:56.435
But you know what?
It really worked for us.

00:03:56.435 --> 00:03:58.537
I mean, my theory was,
"We got a bunch of

00:03:58.537 --> 00:04:00.740
supposedly funny people here.

00:04:00.740 --> 00:04:04.143
I'm paying them, right?"
-Yeah.

00:04:04.143 --> 00:04:06.245
-"There's something in there.
If you said it,

00:04:06.245 --> 00:04:08.347
there's something in there,
and I insist upon it."

00:04:08.347 --> 00:04:09.882
[ Laughter ]
And we did.

00:04:09.882 --> 00:04:11.384
We did a great job with that.
We wrote --

00:04:11.384 --> 00:04:15.154
Some of the funniest sketches
came out of dead-dog pitches.

00:04:15.154 --> 00:04:17.356
-Yeah.
-Just garbage pitches.

00:04:17.356 --> 00:04:19.024
-You obviously knew this season
was the last season

00:04:19.024 --> 00:04:21.227
when you started filming it.
-Yeah.

00:04:21.227 --> 00:04:23.462
-You know, a character
you spent more time with

00:04:23.462 --> 00:04:24.530
than any character
you've ever played?

00:04:24.530 --> 00:04:26.799
-Yeah.
-Did you feel like you had to

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:30.002
emotionally say goodbye as you
filmed the last few episodes?

00:04:30.002 --> 00:04:32.505
-Not really.
I think I'm saying goodbye

00:04:32.505 --> 00:04:35.774
as I watch the final season of
"Better Call Saul" play out.

00:04:35.774 --> 00:04:37.943
I certainly know
where the story goes,

00:04:37.943 --> 00:04:42.748
but there's something about
the closure of watching it,

00:04:42.748 --> 00:04:46.152
because it's you --
if you've been with us,

00:04:46.152 --> 00:04:47.920
if you've been watching
"Better Call Saul,"

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:52.825
I can almost assure you you're
gonna love where it goes.

00:04:52.825 --> 00:04:57.830
The writers did a marvelous job,
and so I know when I watch those

00:04:57.830 --> 00:05:00.967
final scenes is when I'll really
confront the fact

00:05:00.967 --> 00:05:04.070
that this character is past,
for me.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.905
It's been the greatest character
you could dream of.

00:05:06.905 --> 00:05:09.375
It's funny on one page,
and three pages later,

00:05:09.375 --> 00:05:11.310
it's heartbreaking.

00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.280
And so, I don't know,
I got very lucky here.

00:05:14.280 --> 00:05:15.914
-That's amazing.
The last thing I want

00:05:15.914 --> 00:05:18.484
to ask you about, because this
seems like a dream to me.

00:05:18.484 --> 00:05:21.954
Your son is comedy writing,
and the two of you did

00:05:21.954 --> 00:05:24.223
a podcast together
with a lot of your old friends.

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:28.127
-"Summer in Argyle,"
and it's on Audible.

00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:32.597
And it's got David Cross
and Tim Robinson is on it

00:05:32.597 --> 00:05:34.433
and so many funny people.

00:05:34.433 --> 00:05:37.570
Stephanie Courtney,
just the best, Brian Posehn.

00:05:37.570 --> 00:05:39.305
-Do you feel like
you're different when your --

00:05:39.305 --> 00:05:41.640
-It's pure comedy,
and he reminds me

00:05:41.640 --> 00:05:44.310
of working with a young me.
It's like falling through

00:05:44.310 --> 00:05:48.147
a time warp, and it's pure,
insane silliness,

00:05:48.147 --> 00:05:51.150
the kind of stuff
you loved when you were 20.

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:53.519
-So that must make you
intensely proud.

00:05:53.519 --> 00:05:56.556
-It makes me intensely proud
and worried for him.

00:05:56.556 --> 00:05:59.658
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.658 --> 00:06:02.227
-Hey, thank you so much
for all the years of Saul

00:06:02.227 --> 00:06:03.429
and for being here tonight.
Really appreciate it.

00:06:03.429 --> 00:06:04.597
-Thanks, man.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:04.597 --> 00:06:07.299
-Bob Odenkirk.
"Comedy Comedy Comedy Drama"

00:06:07.299 --> 00:06:09.235
is out now, and new episodes
of "Better Call Saul"

00:06:09.235 --> 00:06:11.371
air Mondays on AMC and AMC+.

